window.__docsIndex=[{"u":"00-overview-readme.html","t":"Pare documentation","s":"Overview","h":[{"i":"who-it-is-for","t":"Who it is for"},{"i":"who-it-is-not-for","t":"Who it is not for"},{"i":"where-to-go-next","t":"Where to go next"}],"b":"Pare documentation Pare turns TypeScript into JavaScript by erasing the types. That is all it does. It is written in C#, ships as one native binary, and needs no Node, no npm and no source maps. Here is a file before and after: function add(a: number, b: number): number { return a + b; } function add(a , b ) { return a + b; } The types became spaces. Every other character is still on the same line and column it started on. When the browser says app.js:2:10, that is app.ts:2:10. Who it is for ASP.NET Core developers who want to write TypeScript in wwwroot without installing Node. Add one package and dotnet build does the rest. Anyone with a static site who wants a single binary that turns src/*.ts into js/*.js. Tool authors who want to strip types from C#, or from Rust, Go, Python and others through a C library. Who it is not for Pare does not type check, bundle, minify or compile JSX. If you need those, use tsc, esbuild or Vite. Pare also refuses TypeScript that needs generated code, such as enum. It tells you what to write instead. See What Pare does not do. Where to go next You want to Read Try it in five minutes Getting started Add it to an ASP.NET Core app Your first web app Use it without .NET Your first static site Understand how it works How Pare works See how it compares to esbuild, Node and the rest Pare and the alternatives Look up an error Error codes Look up a setting MSBuild properties"},{"u":"00-overview-comparison.html","t":"Pare and the alternatives","s":"Overview","h":[{"i":"at-a-glance","t":"At a glance"},{"i":"pick-by-situation","t":"Pick by situation"},{"i":"speed","t":"Speed"},{"i":"what-pare-adds-in-net","t":"What Pare adds in .NET"}],"b":"Pare and the alternatives Browsers run JavaScript, so something has to remove the types. Here is where Pare sits next to the other options. At a glance Tool Type checks Rewrites code Needs Output matches source positions Pare no no, erases only nothing (one binary) or .NET yes, always Node 22.18+ type stripping no no, erases only Node yes ts-blank-space no no, erases only Node yes Deno, Bun no yes, so enum works their runtime with source maps esbuild, swc, oxc no yes, can bundle and minify npm or a binary with source maps tsc yes yes Node or the native tsc with source maps Pare and Node's type stripping accept the same subset of TypeScript, the one that erasableSyntaxOnly describes. Code that passes one passes the other. Pick by situation Node, Deno or Bun already runs your code. Let them run the TypeScript. You do not need Pare. You want bundling and minification and npm in your build is fine. Use esbuild or Vite. You build with .NET and want TypeScript in the browser with no Node anywhere (dev laptops, CI, build servers): Pare. You want a standalone converter in a Makefile, Dockerfile or CI step: the pare CLI. You want to strip types from Rust, Go, Python or another language: libpare. Speed The same 2,392 real-world files (19.5 MB: TypeScript's compiler, Vue, Hono, Deno std, Svelte, Zod) through each tool, on an Apple M1 Max: Tool Throughput Pare is Pare 0.5.0 279 MB/s - oxc-transform 0.154.0 87 MB/s 3.2x faster Node 24.16 stripTypeScriptTypes 40 MB/s 7.0x faster ts-blank-space 0.9.0 28 MB/s 10.0x faster esbuild 0.28.2 12 MB/s 23.4x faster Throughput moves 10 to 20 percent between runs on a laptop. The full table, including how closely each tool's output matches Pare's, is in Benchmarks. What Pare adds in .NET In a JavaScript project you would use With Pare Vite or webpack with ts-loader Extrode.Pare.Build, a package reference the Vite dev server, tsc --watch builder.AddPareDevelopment() and dotnet watch //go:embed or bun build --compile for a UI inside the binary <PareEmbed>true<\/PareEmbed> ts.transpileModule() TypeStripper.Strip(...) The older .NET option, Microsoft.TypeScript.MSBuild, needs Node at build time."},{"u":"00-overview-how-it-works.html","t":"How Pare works","s":"Overview","h":[{"i":"what-erase-means","t":"What \"erase\" means"},{"i":"a-few-things-are-not-whitespace","t":"A few things are not whitespace"},{"i":"why-it-is-fast","t":"Why it is fast"},{"i":"where-the-pieces-live","t":"Where the pieces live"},{"i":"inside-a-net-build","t":"Inside a .NET build"}],"b":"How Pare works Pare reads your TypeScript once, finds the parts that are only types, and writes the same text back with those parts replaced by spaces. It never builds a syntax tree and never prints code, so nothing it does not understand can be reformatted or lost. The lexer splits the text into tokens: names, numbers, strings, template literals, regular expressions, comments. The recognizer walks the tokens with TypeScript's own grammar rules (it follows parser.ts) and records which ranges are types. It writes down a list of ranges, not a tree. The eraser copies the source to the output, writing a space for every character in those ranges. Line breaks inside an erased range are kept, so line numbers never change. If the recognizer meets syntax that cannot be erased, such as enum, the file is rejected with an error that names the line and what to write instead. What \"erase\" means You write Pare writes let x: number = 1; let x = 1; function f<T>(a: T): T {} function f (a ) {} value as string value interface A { x: number } (spaces, the line breaks stay) import type { A } from \"m\"; (spaces) import { type A, b } from \"m\"; import { b } from \"m\"; The full list is in Supported syntax. A few things are not whitespace In a handful of cases erasing alone would change what the program means, so Pare adds a character or edits an import: import \"./util.ts\" becomes import \"./util.js\" in the CLI and in the MSBuild package. A line that begins with ( or [ after an erased statement gets a ; so it does not glue onto the line above. Output changes lists every case. Why it is fast The lexer and recognizer work on spans of the original text and write erase ranges into a buffer that is reused. While Pare parses it allocates nothing: no tokens, no strings, no tree. On checker.ts from the TypeScript repository (3.1 MB) it takes about 9 ms, around 335 MB/s. See Benchmarks. Where the pieces live Package What it is Extrode.Pare the library: TypeStripper.Strip. No dependencies. Extrode.Pare.Cli the pare command line tool, NativeAOT Extrode.Pare.Build an MSBuild task and targets that strip .ts during dotnet build Extrode.Pare.AspNetCore AddPareDevelopment(): re-strips on save under dotnet watch libpare the same stripper as a C shared library, for other languages Inside a .NET build The output lives under obj/, never in your source tree, so there is nothing to put in .gitignore. The build keeps the .ts out of the published site and deletes the output of a .ts you delete."},{"u":"00-overview-what-pare-does-not-do.html","t":"What Pare does not do","s":"Overview","h":[{"i":"no-type-checking","t":"No type checking"},{"i":"no-generated-code","t":"No generated code"},{"i":"no-bundling-minifying-jsx-or-downleveling","t":"No bundling, minifying, JSX or downleveling"},{"i":"no-source-maps","t":"No source maps"},{"i":"not-in-the-browser","t":"Not in the browser"}],"b":"What Pare does not do Knowing the limits up front saves time. No type checking A type error goes straight through: let n: number = \"hello\"; // Pare strips this without complaint Use TypeScript's own checker for types. TypeScript 7 ships a native tsc that runs without Node. With PareTypeCheck=true the build runs it for you; see Type checking in CI. No generated code Some TypeScript features need the compiler to write JavaScript that is not in your file. Pare only erases, so it rejects these and tells you what to write instead: Feature Rejected as Write this instead enum, const enum PARE100 a const object with as const namespace with values PARE101 an ES module constructor(private x: number) PARE102 declare the field and assign it import x = require(\"m\") PARE103 import x from \"m\" export = x PARE104 export default x <T>value PARE105 value as T parameter decorators PARE106 decorate the method These are the same cases that \"erasableSyntaxOnly\": true in tsconfig.json reports. Turn it on and your editor warns you as you type. Node's built-in type stripping accepts the same subset, so what Pare accepts also runs under Node. No bundling, minifying, JSX or downleveling The output is your code minus its types. .tsx is not supported. Modern syntax stays as it is, so the browser or runtime has to understand it. If you want one minified file, run a bundler on Pare's output or use esbuild instead. No source maps Not needed: lines and columns do not move. Not in the browser Pare runs on a machine with .NET or through the CLI and C library. There is no WebAssembly build."},{"u":"01-getting-started-readme.html","t":"Getting started","s":"Getting started","h":[{"i":"requirements","t":"Requirements"},{"i":"install","t":"Install"},{"i":"check-that-it-works","t":"Check that it works"}],"b":"Getting started Pick the way you want to use Pare. Each one takes about five minutes. I have... Use Start here an ASP.NET Core app the MSBuild package Your first web app a static site, or any non-.NET project the pare command Your first static site C# code that wants to strip types the library Using the library Rust, Go, Python or similar the C library Using libpare from C and other languages Requirements To use You need the pare command from a release archive nothing the pare command as a .NET tool the .NET 10 SDK (on platforms without a prebuilt binary, the tool runs on the .NET 10 runtime) Extrode.Pare.Build or Extrode.Pare.AspNetCore the .NET 10 SDK, a project that targets net10.0 Visual Studio Visual Studio 2026 (MSBuild 18). Visual Studio 2022 cannot build net10.0 projects. libpare a C compiler or an FFI in your language Pare builds on Windows, macOS and Linux. dotnet build works from the command line, Rider and VS Code. The prebuilt binaries cover osx-arm64, osx-x64, linux-x64, linux-arm64, linux-musl-x64, linux-musl-arm64, win-x64 and win-arm64. Install NuGet packages (for .NET projects): dotnet add package Extrode.Pare.Build dotnet add package Extrode.Pare.AspNetCore Open the project file afterwards and add PrivateAssets=\"all\" to the Extrode.Pare.Build reference, so projects that reference yours do not inherit the build step. The command line tool: Download pare-<version>-<rid>.tar.gz (.zip on Windows) from the releases page, unpack it and put the folder on your PATH. It needs no .NET. Or install the .NET tool: dotnet tool install -g Extrode.Pare.Cli. It needs the .NET 10 runtime and starts slower than the archive. Check that it works echo 'const n: number = 1;' | pare --stdin You should see the line back with the type replaced by spaces: const n = 1;"},{"u":"01-getting-started-first-static-site.html","t":"Your first static site","s":"Getting started","h":[{"i":"1-install-pare","t":"1. Install pare"},{"i":"2-get-the-sample","t":"2. Get the sample"},{"i":"3-strip-it","t":"3. Strip it"},{"i":"4-serve-it","t":"4. Serve it"},{"i":"5-read-the-output","t":"5. Read the output"},{"i":"using-it-on-your-own-project","t":"Using it on your own project"}],"b":"Your first static site You will turn a few TypeScript files into JavaScript with the pare command and open them in a browser. No .NET project, no Node, no bundler. 1. Install pare Download pare-<version>-<rid>.tar.gz for your system from the releases page (.zip on Windows), unpack it and put the folder on your PATH: tar -xzf pare-0.6.3-osx-arm64.tar.gz export PATH=\"$PWD/pare-0.6.3-osx-arm64:$PATH\" pare --version With the .NET 10 runtime installed you can use dotnet tool install -g Extrode.Pare.Cli instead; it starts slower than the archive. 2. Get the sample The repository has a ready-made page in samples/CliSite: three TypeScript modules in src/. main.ts imports the other two and uses interfaces, generics, satisfies, as, ! and a type-only import. git clone https://github.com/extrode/pare cd pare 3. Strip it pare samples/CliSite/src --outdir samples/CliSite/js 4. Serve it Any static server works: python3 -m http.server 8000 -d samples/CliSite Open http://localhost:8000. You should see \"2 of 3 open\" over three tasks sorted by priority, and \"Add task\" adds one. Before you ran pare, the page said \"JavaScript did not run\". 5. Read the output Open samples/CliSite/js/main.js next to src/main.ts. The types are spaces, so every line and column matches. The imports changed from ./model.ts to ./model.js, because browsers load the .js files: import { byPriority, summary, } from \"./model.js\"; const list = document.querySelector(\"#tasks\") ; Using it on your own project pare src # every src/**/*.ts becomes .js next to its source pare src --outdir dist # mirror the folder structure into dist/ pare \"src/**/*.ts\" --check-only # report errors, write nothing pare --stdin < in.ts > out.js # one file from stdin to stdout See The pare command for the rest, including how to re-run it on every save."},{"u":"01-getting-started-first-web-app.html","t":"Your first web app","s":"Getting started","h":[{"i":"1-create-the-project","t":"1. Create the project"},{"i":"2-add-the-packages","t":"2. Add the packages"},{"i":"3-serve-static-files","t":"3. Serve static files"},{"i":"4-write-some-typescript","t":"4. Write some TypeScript"},{"i":"5-build-and-run","t":"5. Build and run"},{"i":"6-edit-and-watch-it-reload","t":"6. Edit and watch it reload"},{"i":"7-see-an-error","t":"7. See an error"},{"i":"8-publish","t":"8. Publish"},{"i":"where-next","t":"Where next"}],"b":"Your first web app You will build a small ASP.NET Core site whose script is written in TypeScript, and see it served as JavaScript. You need the .NET 10 SDK and a terminal. No Node. 1. Create the project dotnet new web -n Hello cd Hello 2. Add the packages dotnet add package Extrode.Pare.Build dotnet add package Extrode.Pare.AspNetCore Open Hello.csproj and mark the build package private. This keeps the build step out of any project that references yours: <ItemGroup> <PackageReference Include=\"Extrode.Pare.Build\" Version=\"0.6.3\" PrivateAssets=\"all\" /> <PackageReference Include=\"Extrode.Pare.AspNetCore\" Version=\"0.6.3\" /> <\/ItemGroup> Use the version dotnet add package wrote for you; 0.6.3 here is only an example. What each does: Extrode.Pare.Build strips every wwwroot/**/*.ts when you run dotnet build. Extrode.Pare.AspNetCore strips again when you save a .ts while dotnet watch runs, so you see the change without a rebuild. 3. Serve static files Replace Program.cs with: using Extrode.Pare.AspNetCore; var builder = WebApplication.CreateBuilder(args); builder.AddPareDevelopment(); // does nothing outside Development var app = builder.Build(); app.UseDefaultFiles(); // \"/\" serves index.html app.UseRouting(); app.MapStaticAssets(); // serves what the build registered, including Pare's output app.Run(); 4. Write some TypeScript Create wwwroot/index.html: <!doctype html> <html lang=\"en\"> <head> <meta charset=\"utf-8\"> <title>Hello, Pare<\/title> <script type=\"module\" src=\"/js/app.js\"><\/script> <\/head> <body> <h1 id=\"greeting\"><\/h1> <\/body> <\/html> The page asks for /js/app.js. You will only write app.ts. Create wwwroot/js/app.ts: interface Person { name: string; } function greet(person: Person): string { return `Hello, ${person.name}!`; } const heading = document.querySelector(\"#greeting\")!; heading.textContent = greet({ name: \"Pare\" }); 5. Build and run dotnet build Look at what the build made: obj/Debug/net10.0/pare/js/app.js That is your script with the types gone. Open it and compare it with app.ts: same lines, same columns. dotnet run Open the address it prints. You should see \"Hello, Pare!\". Your browser asked for /js/app.js and the build had already registered the stripped file as that URL. 6. Edit and watch it reload Stop the app and run it with hot reload: dotnet watch Change \"Pare\" in app.ts to your own name and save. The page reloads with the new text and the app does not restart. dotnet watch runs no build when only a .ts changes. Without AddPareDevelopment() the .js would go stale. That one line is what keeps it fresh. 7. See an error Add this to the end of app.ts, after a blank line: enum Color { Red, Green } Build again: wwwroot/js/app.ts(12,1): error PARE100: 'enum' generates runtime code and cannot be erased (use a 'const' object with 'as const' instead of 'enum') It reads like a C# compiler error and points at the line. Under dotnet watch the same message appears in the log and the old .js keeps being served. Replace the enum with the suggestion and it builds again. All the codes are in Error codes. 8. Publish dotnet publish -c Release The published site holds app.js with .br and .gz copies. The .ts source is not published. Set PareKeepSources=true if you want it there. Where next Scripts live outside wwwroot: TypeScript outside wwwroot Catch type errors in the build: Type checking in CI Understand the hot reload details: Hot reload Every setting: MSBuild properties"},{"u":"03-guides-readme.html","t":"Guides","s":"Guides","h":[],"b":"Guides Task-focused walkthroughs. Each one starts from a working project, so do Getting started first. I want to Guide use pare in a script, Makefile or CI The pare command keep scripts outside wwwroot TypeScript outside wwwroot refresh the browser on every save Hot reload ship TypeScript in a Razor class library Razor class libraries embed the output in a console app or NativeAOT binary Non-web projects fail the build on type errors Type checking in CI restore without nuget.org Offline and vendored packages call Pare from C# Using the library call Pare from Rust, Go, Python, Ruby or PHP Using libpare from C and other languages let another compiler (Husk, for Svelte) use Pare's build Compiling other languages"},{"u":"03-guides-c-library.html","t":"Using libpare from C and other languages","s":"Guides","h":[{"i":"get-it","t":"Get it"},{"i":"a-first-program","t":"A first program"},{"i":"the-api-in-one-table","t":"The API in one table"},{"i":"things-to-know","t":"Things to know"}],"b":"Using libpare from C and other languages libpare is the same stripper as a C shared library. Rust, Go (cgo), Python (ctypes), Ruby, PHP, and Node or Deno FFI can call it in-process. No .NET runtime is needed. Get it Each release has libpare-<version>-<rid>.tar.gz (.zip on Windows). Inside: Platform Files macOS libpare.dylib, pare.h Linux libpare.so, pare.h Windows pare.dll, pare.lib, pare.h or build it yourself: dotnet publish src/Extrode.Pare.Native -r <rid>. A first program #include <stdio.h> #include <string.h> #include \"pare.h\" int main(void) { const char *ts = \"let a: number = 1;\\n\"; pare_result r; if (pare_strip(ts, strlen(ts), 0, &r) == PARE_OK) fputs(r.output, stdout); /* \"let a = 1;\\n\" */ else for (size_t i = 0; i < r.diagnostic_count; i++) fprintf(stderr, \"app.ts:%d:%d: error PARE%03d: %s\\n\", r.diagnostics[i].line, r.diagnostics[i].column, r.diagnostics[i].code, pare_message(r.diagnostics[i].code)); pare_free(&r); } Build and run: cc app.c -I<dir> -L<dir> -lpare -Wl,-rpath,<dir> # macOS, Linux cl app.c /I<dir> <dir>\\pare.lib # Windows; pare.dll next to app.exe <dir> is the folder you unpacked. The API in one table Function Does pare_strip(source, length, options, &result) strips; returns a status code pare_free(&result) frees and zeroes the result; safe on a zeroed result pare_message(code), pare_hint(code) static text for a diagnostic code pare_version() the library version, such as \"0.6.3\" pare_abi_version() compare with PARE_ABI_VERSION to catch a header and library from different builds Status codes: PARE_OK (0), PARE_REJECTED (1), PARE_INVALID (2), PARE_INTERNAL (3). The full contract is in the C API reference. Things to know UTF-8 in and out. A leading BOM is dropped, as the CLI does. Invalid UTF-8 returns PARE_INVALID. Positions. Lines and UTF-16 columns are kept, so the output's line:column is the source's. Byte offsets shift when a blanked type holds non-ASCII text. Diagnostics carry the code, line, UTF-16 column (what browsers report), and the byte offset and length into the source. Thread-safe. The library allocates and pare_free frees. It cannot be unloaded. Several NativeAOT libraries in one process are fine. On linux-musl-arm64, call it from a thread you created; see Using the library."},{"u":"03-guides-cli.html","t":"The pare command","s":"Guides","h":[{"i":"commands","t":"Commands"},{"i":"relative-imports","t":"Relative imports"},{"i":"exit-codes","t":"Exit codes"},{"i":"strip-again-on-every-save","t":"Strip again on every save"},{"i":"in-ci","t":"In CI"},{"i":"install-options","t":"Install options"}],"b":"The pare command pare turns .ts files into .js files by erasing the types. It does nothing else: no bundling, no minifying, no type checking, no Node packages. Commands Command What it does pare src Writes each src/**/*.ts as .js next to its source. .mts becomes .mjs, .cts becomes .cjs. pare src --outdir js Writes under js/ instead, keeping the folder structure below src/. pare src --check-only Reports errors and writes nothing. Use it in CI or a pre-commit hook. pare --stdin < in.ts > out.js Reads one file from stdin and writes JavaScript to stdout. For editors and pipelines. pare --version Prints the version. Inputs can be files, folders (searched recursively) or globs (*, ?, **) for shells that do not expand them. .d.ts files and node_modules are skipped. An existing foo.js next to foo.ts is replaced, like tsc and esbuild do. Keep hand-written JavaScript out of the source folders, or use --outdir. $ cat in.ts const n: number = 1 as const; $ pare --stdin < in.ts const n = 1 ; Relative imports Browsers and Node load .js files, so Pare rewrites relative specifiers that name a TypeScript file: ./util.ts becomes ./util.js, .mts becomes .mjs, .cts becomes .cjs. This covers import, export ... from and import(\"...\"). Exit codes Code Meaning 0 Every file was stripped (or checked). 1 A file uses syntax that cannot be erased. That file gets no output; the message names the line. 2 I/O or usage error, for example a missing input. $ pare --check-only bad.ts bad.ts:1:1: error PARE100: 'enum' generates runtime code and cannot be erased (use a 'const' object with 'as const' instead of 'enum') $ pare missing.ts pare: missing.ts: no such file or directory Every rejection in a run is reported, not only the first. Strip again on every save pare has no watch mode. Let Node or Deno watch the folder and run it. The sample has a small script, samples/CliSite/watch.mjs, that runs pare once and prints errors without stopping: node --watch-path=samples/CliSite/src samples/CliSite/watch.mjs deno run --watch=samples/CliSite/src --allow-run=pare --allow-env samples/CliSite/watch.mjs Copy the script into your project and change the paths. In CI pare src --check-only fails the job with exit code 1 if any file uses syntax Pare cannot erase. To also check types, run tsc --noEmit after it; see Type checking in CI. Install options dotnet tool install -g Extrode.Pare.Cli installs one framework-dependent tool for every platform. It needs the .NET 10 runtime and starts slower than the NativeAOT binary. The NativeAOT binaries for osx-arm64, osx-x64, linux-arm64, linux-x64, linux-musl-arm64, linux-musl-x64, win-x64 and win-arm64 come as archives on the releases page and need no .NET."},{"u":"03-guides-compile-other-languages.html","t":"Compiling other languages","s":"Guides","h":[{"i":"declare-the-sources-and-your-target","t":"Declare the sources and your target"},{"i":"hot-reload","t":"Hot reload"}],"b":"Compiling other languages For authors of build packages. Pare's plumbing, the part that puts output under obj/, serves it as a static web asset, publishes it compressed, and deletes it with its source, works for any compile-to-JavaScript step. Husk, which compiles Svelte components, uses it. If you only write TypeScript you can skip this page. Declare the sources and your target Usually in your package's .props and .targets: <ItemGroup> <PareCompileSource Include=\"Components/**/*.svelte\" PareCompiler=\"husk\" PareOutputExtension=\".svelte.js\" PareServeAt=\"/\" /> <\/ItemGroup> <PropertyGroup> <PareCompileDependsOn>$(PareCompileDependsOn);HuskCompile<\/PareCompileDependsOn> <\/PropertyGroup> <Target Name=\"HuskCompile\" Inputs=\"@(PareCompileSource)\" Outputs=\"@(PareCompileSource->'%(PareOutputPath)')\"> <!-- write each source's output to %(PareOutputPath) --> <\/Target> Metadata PareCompiler required: the compiler's name, the same as its IPareCompiler.Name PareOutputExtension required: the output's name is the source's name without its extension, plus this (App.svelte + .svelte.js = App.svelte.js) PareServeAt as for TypeScriptSource Targets in PareCompileDependsOn run inside PareStripTypeScript, after the strip and before the outputs are registered. Each @(PareCompileSource) then has PareOutputPath (where to write; relative paths are below the project) and PareRelativePath (its URL or resource name). Skipping up-to-date sources is the target's job (Inputs/Outputs). Two outputs from one source (a component's .js and .css) are two items with the same Include and different extensions. Every file the target writes under obj/ must be one of these outputs: the build deletes any other file there as a deleted source's output (a .js.map it was not told about, say). The sources leave Content, so a .svelte under wwwroot is not published. They are Watch items. @(PareOutput) lists their outputs, with %(PareCompiler). error PARE204 for a missing PareCompiler (letters, digits, ., _, -) or PareOutputExtension. PARE201, PARE202 and PARE203 apply as for TypeScript, across both kinds of source. Hot reload On save in Development, AddPareDevelopment() hands each such source to the IPareCompiler with its name, registered in DI: builder.Services.AddSingleton<IPareCompiler, SvelteCompiler>(); builder.AddPareDevelopment(); // a second call, from another package's setup, adds no watcher; its options apply too CompileAsync(sourcePath, source, outputPaths, logger, cancellationToken) returns the text of each output keyed by path, or null after logging its errors (the old outputs stay). Pare calls each compiler one source at a time, writes what changed and deletes the outputs of a deleted source. Sources out of date at start are compiled before the app serves. A source whose compiler is not registered gets a warning at start and is not compiled. The decision behind this is decision 005 (docs/decisions/ in the repository)."},{"u":"03-guides-hot-reload.html","t":"Hot reload","s":"Guides","h":[{"i":"turn-it-on","t":"Turn it on"},{"i":"limits","t":"Limits"},{"i":"if-it-does-not-reload","t":"If it does not reload"}],"b":"Hot reload dotnet watch reloads the browser when a file changes. For C# that works out of the box. For .ts it needs one line, because in its default hot reload mode dotnet watch runs no build when a .ts file is saved, so the .js would go stale. Turn it on <PackageReference Include=\"Extrode.Pare.AspNetCore\" Version=\"0.6.3\" /> using Extrode.Pare.AspNetCore; builder.AddPareDevelopment(); // before builder.Build(); does nothing outside Development Now this happens on every save: At start-up the app strips every source that is out of date. It reads Extrode.Pare.development.json, which the build puts next to the app and never publishes, to know where each source's output goes. A rejected file is logged as file:line:col: error PARE... and the old .js stays. Deleting a source deletes its output under obj/. It covers sources served with PareServeAt and sources of a Razor class library that the app references as a project. If you set PareOutDir, pass the same folder: builder.AddPareDevelopment(o => o.OutDir = \"...\"). AddPareDevelopment also adds dotnet watch's browser refresh to WebApplication.CreateSlimBuilder apps, which never run the hosting startup that adds it. Limits A .ts file added while dotnet watch runs is stripped, but the page gets 404 for it until the next build. Press Ctrl+R in dotnet watch to rebuild. The log says so. A request for a deleted source's output answers 500 in Development until the next build. In Development the server still serves .ts source files on disk with a warning. MapStaticAssets serves any file on disk in Development, and UseStaticFiles serves everything under wwwroot. Published output never contains them. If it does not reload PublishAot project: add <StartupHookSupport Condition=\"'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug'\">true<\/StartupHookSupport>. Without it dotnet watch says \"Project does not support Hot Reload\". Run dotnet watch from the project directory. With --project from another folder, .NET SDK 10.0.401 on macOS fails to read obj\\Debug/.../staticwebassets.development.json and never refreshes the page. No package? dotnet watch --no-hot-reload rebuilds and restarts on every save (about 3 seconds on the sample) and you reload the page yourself."},{"u":"03-guides-library.html","t":"Using the library","s":"Guides","h":[{"i":"strip-a-string","t":"Strip a string"},{"i":"strip-without-allocating","t":"Strip without allocating"},{"i":"why-utf-16","t":"Why UTF-16"},{"i":"test-typescript-modules-in-a-net-javascript-engine","t":"Test TypeScript modules in a .NET JavaScript engine"},{"i":"deep-nesting","t":"Deep nesting"}],"b":"Using the library Extrode.Pare is the stripper alone: no MSBuild, no package dependencies. dotnet add package Extrode.Pare Strip a string using Extrode.Pare; string? js = TypeStripper.Strip(source, out IReadOnlyList<Diagnostic> diagnostics); if (js is null) { foreach (var d in diagnostics) Console.Error.WriteLine(DiagnosticFormatter.Format(\"app.ts\", source, d)); } js is null when the file was rejected. This overload allocates, which is fine for tests and tools. Strip without allocating bool ok = TypeStripper.Strip(source, output, diagnostics); bool ok2 = TypeStripper.Strip(source, output, diagnostics, StripOptions.RewriteRelativeImportExtensions); Argument Type source ReadOnlySpan<char> output an IBufferWriter<char> diagnostics an IDiagnosticSink StripOptions.RewriteRelativeImportExtensions turns ./x.ts into ./x.js. The library leaves it off by default; the CLI and the MSBuild package turn it on. These overloads, Diagnostic, DiagnosticCode, IDiagnosticSink, DiagnosticFormatter and StripOptions are stable for 0.x. Later 0.x releases add and never change or remove, and DiagnosticCode values are fixed. Why UTF-16 JavaScript engines report columns in UTF-16 code units, so erasing in UTF-16 (one space per code unit) keeps every position the same by construction. The reasoning is recorded in decision 001 (docs/decisions/ in the repository). Test TypeScript modules in a .NET JavaScript engine You can run your TypeScript modules in Jint with no build output and no Node. JintModuleTests.cs is a 25-line Jint module loader that strips each .ts as it is imported (./x.js loads x.ts) and reports rejections as file:line:col: error PARE.... Deep nesting Very deep nesting fails with a diagnostic instead of a stack overflow. On linux-musl-arm64 NativeAOT, call Strip from a thread you created: .NET reads the main thread's stack size wrongly there and the check fails at once."},{"u":"03-guides-non-web-projects.html","t":"Non-web projects","s":"Guides","h":[{"i":"do-it-your-own-way","t":"Do it your own way"}],"b":"Non-web projects A console app, or a NativeAOT binary that serves its own UI, has no static web assets. Embed the output in the assembly instead. <PropertyGroup> <PareEmbed>true<\/PareEmbed> <PareEmbedPrefix>ui/<\/PareEmbedPrefix> <EnablePareDefaultItems>false<\/EnablePareDefaultItems> <\/PropertyGroup> <ItemGroup> <PackageReference Include=\"Extrode.Pare.Build\" Version=\"0.6.3\" PrivateAssets=\"all\" /> <TypeScriptSource Include=\"web/**/*.ts\" /> <\/ItemGroup> web/lib/util.ts is written to obj/<configuration>/<tfm>/pare/web/lib/util.js, never into the source tree, and embedded as ui/web/lib/util.js. Read it back: using var stream = typeof(Program).Assembly.GetManifestResourceStream(\"ui/web/lib/util.js\"); The resource name is the prefix plus the path below the project. PareServeAt changes the path: <TypeScriptSource Include=\"web/**/*.ts\" PareServeAt=\"/\" /> gives ui/lib/util.js. The outputs are not Content, not static web assets, and not copied to the build or publish output. A name such as strings.de.ts stays in the main assembly (no satellite assembly for de). The step is incremental: a .ts edit rewrites its .js and recompiles; a deleted .ts drops its resource and its .js on the next build. Do it your own way To embed or copy the files differently, leave PareEmbed off and use the @(PareOutput) item: <PropertyGroup> <PareOutDir>$(BaseIntermediateOutputPath)pare/<\/PareOutDir> <\/PropertyGroup> <Target Name=\"EmbedPare\" AfterTargets=\"PareStripTypeScript\"> <ItemGroup> <EmbeddedResource Include=\"@(PareOutput)\" LogicalName=\"ui/%(PareOutput.PareRelativePath)\" /> <\/ItemGroup> <\/Target> $(IntermediateOutputPath) is still empty where a project file sets its properties, so PareOutDir cannot use it. $(BaseIntermediateOutputPath) (obj/) is set by then. After PareStripTypeScript, @(PareOutput) lists every output. %(PareSource) is the source's full path. %(PareRelativePath) uses /: the URL path for a static web asset (js/app.js), the resource name without the prefix under PareEmbed, and otherwise the output's path below the project (or below PareOutDir). A target with AfterTargets=\"PareStripTypeScript\" sees it on every build, including builds that skip the strip as up to date."},{"u":"03-guides-offline-restore.html","t":"Offline and vendored packages","s":"Guides","h":[],"b":"Offline and vendored packages If a machine cannot reach nuget.org, or you want the exact .nupkg files in your repo, vendor them. Each GitHub release carries the packages. Put them in a folder next to the solution: packages/Extrode.Pare.0.6.3.nupkg packages/Extrode.Pare.Build.0.6.3.nupkg and add a nuget.config beside the solution (the path is relative to that file): <configuration> <packageSources> <add key=\"nuget.org\" value=\"https://api.nuget.org/v3/index.json\" /> <add key=\"vendored\" value=\"packages\" /> <\/packageSources> <packageSourceMapping> <packageSource key=\"nuget.org\"><package pattern=\"*\" /><\/packageSource> <packageSource key=\"vendored\"><package pattern=\"Extrode.Pare*\" /><\/packageSource> <\/packageSourceMapping> <\/configuration> Pin an exact release (Version=\"0.6.3\"), not a -dev or -* version. The CLI tool package works the same way: put the Extrode.Pare.Cli.*.nupkg files in one folder and run dotnet tool install -g Extrode.Pare.Cli --add-source ./packages. If your project uses a nuget.config that you do not want published, mark it so: <Content Update=\"nuget.config\" CopyToPublishDirectory=\"Never\" />."},{"u":"03-guides-razor-class-libraries.html","t":"Razor class libraries","s":"Guides","h":[{"i":"hot-reload","t":"Hot reload"}],"b":"Razor class libraries Reference Extrode.Pare.Build from the library. Apps that use the library need nothing. <PackageReference Include=\"Extrode.Pare.Build\" Version=\"0.6.3\" PrivateAssets=\"all\" /> The library's wwwroot/js/widget.ts is then: served to apps as /_content/<Library>/js/widget.js; published with compressed copies; packed by dotnet pack as the stripped .js only, with no dependency on Pare. People who use your library never need to know Pare exists. Hot reload When an app references the library as a project, AddPareDevelopment() in the app re-strips the library's sources on save too. Each library's build output carries an Extrode.Pare.development.<Library>.json that reaches the app's."},{"u":"03-guides-type-checking.html","t":"Type checking in CI","s":"Guides","h":[{"i":"let-the-build-run-it","t":"Let the build run it"},{"i":"get-tsc-without-npm","t":"Get tsc without npm"},{"i":"a-tsconfig-that-matches-pare","t":"A tsconfig that matches Pare"},{"i":"without-the-build","t":"Without the build"}],"b":"Type checking in CI Pare does not check types. A line like let n: number = \"hi\" strips without complaint. Type checking is TypeScript's job, and TypeScript 7 ships a native tsc that needs no Node. Let the build run it <PareTypeCheck>true<\/PareTypeCheck> Before the strip, the build runs tsc --noEmit -p tsconfig.json. A type error fails the build. Get tsc without npm scripts/get-tsc.cs in the Pare repository fetches the native tsc from the npm registry and checks its sha512, without npm or Node. Copy it into your repo, outside any project directory (a project compiles every .cs below it), and run it on each machine and in CI before the build: dotnet run scripts/get-tsc.cs --dest .tools/typescript # add .tools/ to .gitignore Tell the build where it is: <PropertyGroup> <PareTypeCheck>true<\/PareTypeCheck> <PareTscPath>$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory).tools/typescript/tsc<\/PareTscPath> <PareTscPath Condition=\"$([MSBuild]::IsOSPlatform('Windows'))\">$(PareTscPath).exe<\/PareTscPath> <\/PropertyGroup> The binary is per OS and CPU (@typescript/typescript-<os>-<cpu>: darwin-arm64, win32-x64, linux-x64 and the other arm64/x64 pairs). Fetch it on each machine instead of committing it. Running it again with the same version does nothing. --version <x.y.z> picks another. The script refuses a --dest that is not empty and that it did not fill itself. $(PareTsconfig) defaults to the project's tsconfig.json. It is never copied to the build or publish output. A tsconfig that matches Pare { \"compilerOptions\": { \"target\": \"esnext\", \"module\": \"esnext\", \"moduleResolution\": \"bundler\", \"lib\": [\"esnext\", \"dom\", \"dom.iterable\"], \"strict\": true, \"noEmit\": true, \"isolatedModules\": true, \"verbatimModuleSyntax\": true, \"erasableSyntaxOnly\": true, \"allowImportingTsExtensions\": true }, \"include\": [\"wwwroot/js/**/*.ts\"] } erasableSyntaxOnly makes the editor and tsc flag the same syntax Pare rejects. verbatimModuleSyntax makes you write import type for types, which is what a stripper needs: without type information Pare cannot tell that an imported name is only a type. Without the build pare src --check-only # syntax Pare cannot erase tsc --noEmit # types"},{"u":"03-guides-typescript-outside-wwwroot.html","t":"TypeScript outside wwwroot","s":"Guides","h":[{"i":"the-one-line-version","t":"The one-line version"},{"i":"blazor-components","t":"Blazor components"},{"i":"how-the-url-is-built","t":"How the URL is built"},{"i":"when-something-is-wrong","t":"When something is wrong"},{"i":"during-development","t":"During development"}],"b":"TypeScript outside wwwroot By default only wwwroot/**/*.ts is served. If your scripts live in Scripts/ or next to your Razor components, tell Pare which URL folder to serve them under with PareServeAt. The one-line version <TypeScriptSource Include=\"Scripts/**/*.ts;Scripts/**/*.mts\" PareServeAt=\"js\" /> The file name and extension come from the source. The folders matched by ** are kept: Source Served at Scripts/app.ts /js/app.js Scripts/lib/fmt.ts /js/lib/fmt.js Scripts/worker.mts /js/worker.mjs The output goes under obj/ like a wwwroot source's: served from the first build, compressed and published. Blazor components A script beside its component, the way the SDK serves a hand-written Counter.razor.js: <TypeScriptSource Include=\"**/*.razor.ts\" Exclude=\"bin/**;obj/**;node_modules/**\" PareServeAt=\"/\" /> Components/Pages/Counter.razor.ts is served at /Components/Pages/Counter.razor.js. How the URL is built / is the root. Leading and trailing slashes are ignored, and \\ counts as /. The URL is the folder, then the part of the path matched by ** in the Include that created the item, then the file name. Without ** (Scripts/*.ts) there is no subfolder. In a Razor class library the folder is below /_content/<Library>/, like wwwroot. A wwwroot source with PareServeAt is served there instead of at its wwwroot path. When something is wrong You see Meaning Fix error PARE203 PareServeAt is not a folder (.., :, //, or a file name like js/app.js) give a folder such as js, or / error PARE201 two sources map to one URL change one PareServeAt, or remove one source warning PARE202 a source outside wwwroot has no PareServeAt, so its .js is written beside it and not served add PareServeAt, or add PARE202 to NoWarn if that is what you want PARE202 is not reported for .razor.ts and .cshtml.ts. See Error codes. During development AddPareDevelopment() re-strips these on save. A new file outside wwwroot is stripped and served after the next build, because static web assets are fixed at build time. Press Ctrl+R in dotnet watch to rebuild."},{"u":"06-reference-readme.html","t":"Reference","s":"Reference","h":[],"b":"Reference Page What is in it Error codes every PARE code, with the fix MSBuild properties settings, item metadata, where output goes Supported syntax what Pare erases, keeps and refuses Output changes the places where output differs from input by more than spaces C API pare.h, function by function Benchmarks speed, agreement with other tools, the Deno std check Troubleshooting symptoms and fixes License the license in plain words"},{"u":"06-reference-benchmarks.html","t":"Benchmarks","s":"Reference","h":[{"i":"library-speed","t":"Library speed"},{"i":"against-other-tools","t":"Against other tools"},{"i":"real-world-check","t":"Real-world check"},{"i":"reproduce","t":"Reproduce"}],"b":"Benchmarks Measured on an Apple M1 Max (osx-arm64), .NET 10.0, Release build. Library speed BenchmarkDotNet 0.15.8, one TypeStripper.Strip call per source, output to a reused buffer: Input Mean MB/s Allocated TypeScript 5.9.3 checker.ts (3.1 MB) 9.3 ms 335 0 B Real-world corpus, 2,392 files (19.5 MB) 60.2 ms 272 0 B (a = nested 1,000 deep 32.3 ms 0.2 0 B Deep nesting is quadratic, as in TypeScript's own parser. Real code never reaches it. Against other tools The same 2,392 files (TypeScript's compiler, Vue, Hono, Deno std, Svelte, Zod at pinned commits) through other tools. Each tool's output is compared with Pare's byte for byte, then as an Acornima syntax tree without locations. Printers (esbuild, oxc, Deno) reprint literals, so the tree comparison ignores literal spelling, { a: a } versus { a } and void 0 versus undefined. For printers, a looser comparison also ignores imports (they drop unused ones) and a numeric suffix on names (they rename a shadowed x to x1). Deno is Deno's own transpiler (swc, as deno run uses it), read from its cache after deno cache --no-check. Tool Same bytes Same tree Same but imports dropped or names renamed Differ Throughput Pare is Pare 0.5.0 - - - - 279 MB/s - Node 24.16 stripTypeScriptTypes 1,898 490 - 0 40 MB/s 7.0x faster oxc-transform 0.154.0 13 2,375 0 0 87 MB/s 3.2x faster ts-blank-space 0.9.0 1,967 420 - 1 28 MB/s 10.0x faster esbuild 0.28.2 11 1,961 296 120 12 MB/s 23.4x faster Deno 2.9.7 (swc) 11 2,326 51 0 - - Same tree but not the same bytes: Node and ts-blank-space keep tabs and write UTF-8-width spaces for non-ASCII comment text, and Pare writes its semicolon fixes in a few places they leave blank. ts-blank-space's one difference is a bug on its side: get!: T followed by another field on the next line becomes a getter, and its output does not parse. Pare had the same bug until this comparison found it. Deno's 51: it drops imports whose names are unused, including import {} from \"m\", which still loads m (Pare keeps it, as verbatimModuleSyntax requires), and renames shadowed bindings. esbuild's 120: it turns strings with line breaks into template literals and folds some expressions. None is a Pare error. Four files are left out: their output does not parse for any stripper, because they import or re-export a type without type, which needs type information to remove. Whole process over all files: the pare CLI (NativeAOT) 0.95 s, deno cache --no-check from a cold cache 1.1 s, one run each. Throughput is the best of 5 passes in one run on a laptop and moves by 10 to 20 percent between runs. The BenchmarkDotNet numbers above are the steady ones. Real-world check scripts/run-std-tests.cs runs Deno's standard library test suite on Pare's output. It strips a copy of denoland/std (pinned commit, 1,600+ files) with the pare CLI in about 0.5 s, points the @std/* imports at the .js files, and runs deno test on each package's original *_test.ts and stripped *_test.js: 38 of 42 packages give the same result (4,997 passing tests). 5,521 tests pass on Pare's output in all. csv: one test compares Function.prototype.toString() with Deno's reprinted (n)=>n. A blanking stripper keeps (n ) => n. testing: snapshot tests look for a snapshot file named after the .ts test file. http: one test passes only on the .js copy, because the TypeScript copy sits under a dot directory (.corpus) that serveDir() refuses. expect: not run. Pare rejects three of its files (parameter properties and a <T>x assertion, which generate runtime code), so expect.js is missing. Failures common to both runs (cli, encoding, fmt, fs and others) come from the environment. Reproduce dotnet run scripts/get-bench-corpus.cs # ~25 MB into .corpus/realworld npm --prefix tests/Extrode.Pare.Bench/compare ci --ignore-scripts dotnet publish src/Extrode.Pare.Cli -c Release -r osx-arm64 -o tmp/aot dotnet run -c Release --project tests/Extrode.Pare.Bench -- compare --passes 5 dotnet run -c Release --project tests/Extrode.Pare.Bench -- --filter '*' dotnet run scripts/run-std-tests.cs # or name packages: ... -- yaml semver compare writes every tool's output under tmp/compare/ and the differences to tmp/compare/differences.tsv."},{"u":"06-reference-c-api.html","t":"C API","s":"Reference","h":[{"i":"functions","t":"Functions"},{"i":"pare_strip","t":"pare_strip"},{"i":"options","t":"Options"},{"i":"types","t":"Types"},{"i":"pare_free","t":"pare_free"},{"i":"pare_message-pare_hint","t":"pare_message, pare_hint"},{"i":"pare_version-pare_abi_version","t":"pare_version, pare_abi_version"},{"i":"positions","t":"Positions"},{"i":"threads-and-loading","t":"Threads and loading"}],"b":"C API The header is pare.h. For a walkthrough see Using libpare from C and other languages. Functions int32_t pare_strip(const char *source, size_t length, uint32_t options, pare_result *result); void pare_free(pare_result *result); const char *pare_message(int32_t code); const char *pare_hint(int32_t code); const char *pare_version(void); uint32_t pare_abi_version(void); pare_strip Strips length bytes of UTF-8 TypeScript at source. NULL with length 0 is the empty source. A leading BOM is dropped. Free *result with pare_free whatever the return value. Return Name Meaning 0 PARE_OK stripped; result->output holds the JavaScript 1 PARE_REJECTED syntax error or non-erasable syntax; see result->diagnostics 2 PARE_INVALID NULL result, NULL source with length > 0, unknown option bits, invalid UTF-8, or length over 2,147,483,591 bytes; nothing allocated 3 PARE_INTERNAL a bug in Pare; nothing allocated Options Flag Does PARE_REWRITE_RELATIVE_IMPORT_EXTENSIONS (1) rewrites relative ./x.ts imports to ./x.js (.mts and .cts likewise), as TypeScript's rewriteRelativeImportExtensions does Pass 0 for no options. Unknown bits return PARE_INVALID. Types typedef struct pare_diagnostic { int32_t code; /* 1 syntax error; 100-107 non-erasable syntax */ int32_t line; /* 1-based */ int32_t column; /* 1-based, in UTF-16 code units */ int32_t start; /* byte offset into the source passed in */ int32_t length; /* bytes */ } pare_diagnostic; typedef struct pare_result { char *output; /* UTF-8, NUL-terminated, no BOM; NULL unless PARE_OK */ size_t output_length; /* bytes, without the NUL */ pare_diagnostic *diagnostics; /* in source order; NULL when there are none */ size_t diagnostic_count; } pare_result; pare_diagnostic is five int32_t, 20 bytes with no padding. output_length is never more than the source's length, and the output holds any NUL the source had. Diagnostic codes are the numbers in Error codes: 1 is a syntax error and 100 to 107 are non-erasable syntax. pare_free Frees what pare_strip allocated and zeroes all four fields of *result. NULL or an already zeroed result is a no-op. pare_message, pare_hint Static strings, never freed. An unknown code gives \"unknown diagnostic\" and \"\". pare_version, pare_abi_version pare_version() returns the library version as a static string. pare_abi_version() returns the PARE_ABI_VERSION the library was built with. Compare it with the macro from your header to refuse a library built for another layout. It is currently 1. Positions Lines and UTF-16 columns are kept, so the output's line:column is the source's. Byte offsets shift when a blanked type contains non-ASCII text, because Pare writes one space per UTF-16 code unit. Threads and loading Every function is thread-safe. The library cannot be unloaded once loaded. Several NativeAOT libraries in one process are fine. On linux-musl-arm64, call pare_strip from a thread you created: there the main thread's stack check can misreport."},{"u":"06-reference-error-codes.html","t":"Error codes","s":"Reference","h":[{"i":"pare001","t":"PARE001"},{"i":"pare100","t":"PARE100"},{"i":"pare101","t":"PARE101"},{"i":"pare102","t":"PARE102"},{"i":"pare103","t":"PARE103"},{"i":"pare104","t":"PARE104"},{"i":"pare105","t":"PARE105"},{"i":"pare106","t":"PARE106"},{"i":"pare107","t":"PARE107"},{"i":"pare200","t":"PARE200"},{"i":"pare201","t":"PARE201"},{"i":"pare202","t":"PARE202"},{"i":"pare203","t":"PARE203"},{"i":"pare204","t":"PARE204"}],"b":"Error codes Pare only erases types: it replaces type syntax with spaces and changes nothing else, so every line and column of the output matches the source. TypeScript syntax that would need new JavaScript written in its place cannot be erased, so Pare rejects it instead of guessing. This is the rule behind TypeScript's erasableSyntaxOnly option and Node's built-in type stripping: turning on erasableSyntaxOnly in tsconfig.json makes tsc and the editor report the PARE100 to PARE105 cases as TS1294. Pare also rejects parameter decorators (PARE106), which that option allows. A rejected file gets no output. The CLI exits with 1, the MSBuild package fails the build, and AddPareDevelopment logs the error and keeps the previous .js. Every rejection in a file is reported in one run. Code Kind Message PARE001 error syntax error PARE100 error 'enum' generates runtime code and cannot be erased PARE101 error namespace containing values generates runtime code PARE102 error parameter property generates runtime code PARE103 error 'import x = ...' generates runtime code PARE104 error 'export =' is CommonJS-only emit PARE105 error angle-bracket type assertion is not supported PARE106 error parameter decorators need legacy experimentalDecorators PARE107 error a type-only import cannot have both a default and named bindings PARE200 warning (MSBuild) not served: the build now writes this file's output under obj/ and serves that. Delete it. PARE201 error (MSBuild) two sources map to one URL PARE202 warning (MSBuild) not served: outside wwwroot with no PareServeAt PARE203 error (MSBuild) PareServeAt is not a URL folder PARE204 error (MSBuild) PareCompileSource needs PareCompiler and PareOutputExtension Errors print as file:line:col: error PARE100: message (hint) from the CLI and the hot-reload watcher, and as file(line,col): error PARE100: message (hint) from MSBuild. PARE001 syntax error (check the syntax at this position) The input is not valid TypeScript at this position. Pare stops at the first syntax error; the position is the token where parsing failed. Nesting too deep for the parser's stack (thousands of levels) is reported the same way rather than crashing. .tsx and JSX are not supported. let total = ; Fix the syntax: let total = 0; PARE100 'enum' generates runtime code and cannot be erased (use a 'const' object with 'as const' instead of 'enum') An enum compiles to an object built at run time, so it cannot be erased. const enum is rejected too: tsc inlines its members at each use, which an eraser cannot do. declare enum emits nothing and is erased. enum Color { Red, Green } Use an object with as const, and a type of the same name for its values: const Color = { Red: 0, Green: 1 } as const; type Color = (typeof Color)[keyof typeof Color]; let c: Color = Color.Red; PARE101 namespace containing values generates runtime code (use an ES module instead of a namespace) A namespace or module block that declares values (variables, functions, classes, nested namespaces with values) compiles to an object built at run time. A namespace that holds only types, or one marked declare, is erased. namespace Util { export const version = 1; } Use a module: export the values from the file, and keep namespaces for types only: export const version = 1; namespace Shapes { export type Kind = \"circle\" | \"square\"; } let k: Shapes.Kind = \"circle\"; PARE102 parameter property generates runtime code (declare the field in the class body and assign it in the constructor) A constructor parameter with public, private, protected, readonly or override also declares a field and assigns it, which needs code that is not in the source. class Point { constructor(public x: number) {} } Declare the field and assign it yourself: class Point { x: number; constructor(x: number) { this.x = x; } } PARE103 'import x = ...' generates runtime code (use an ES 'import' declaration) import x = require(\"m\") is CommonJS emit, and import x = N.y creates a variable. import type x = require(\"m\") is erased. import fs = require(\"node:fs\"); Use an ES import, or import type when only types are used: import fs from \"node:fs\"; import type Fs = require(\"node:fs\"); PARE104 'export =' is CommonJS-only emit (use 'export default' instead) export = compiles to module.exports = ..., which has no ES module form. function greet() {} export = greet; function greet() {} export default greet; PARE105 angle-bracket type assertion is not supported (use 'expr as T' instead of '<T>expr') <T>expr is the older assertion form. It reads like JSX and is not allowed in .tsx files, so Pare accepts only the as form, which every TypeScript file can use. declare const input: unknown; let n = <number>input; declare const input: unknown; let n = input as number; PARE106 parameter decorators need legacy experimentalDecorators (use TC39 decorators on the class or member instead) Decorators on parameters exist only in TypeScript's legacy experimentalDecorators mode, whose emit rewrites the class. Standard (TC39) decorators on classes and members are kept as written; the runtime has to support them. declare function log(target: object, key: string, index: number): void; class Api { get(@log id: string) {} } Decorate the method instead: declare function log(value: Function, context: ClassMethodDecoratorContext): void; class Api { @log get(id: string) {} } PARE107 a type-only import cannot have both a default and named bindings (split it into two 'import type' declarations) TypeScript rejects this form too (TS1363). import type Config, { Options } from \"./config.ts\"; import type Config from \"./config.ts\"; import type { Options } from \"./config.ts\"; PARE200 warning PARE200: not served: the build now writes this file's output under obj/ and serves that. Delete it. MSBuild package only, for web projects whose output goes under obj/ (the default since 0.3). A foo.js sits next to foo.ts in wwwroot: left there by Pare 0.2, which wrote output next to the source, or written by hand. The build serves and publishes the stripped foo.ts from obj/ and leaves foo.js out, so the two cannot conflict. The same holds for another compiler's output (App.svelte.js next to App.svelte). The build still succeeds. Delete the foo.js files the warning names, and any .gitignore rules added for them. If a hand-written foo.js is the one you want served, rename it or the .ts. PARE201 error PARE201: two sources map to one URL, js/app.js: wwwroot/js/app.ts, Scripts/app.ts. Serve one under a different PareServeAt folder, or remove one from its item. MSBuild package only, for web projects whose output goes under obj/. Each source's output is served at a URL: its path below wwwroot by default, or below the folder its PareServeAt names. Two sources ended up with the same one, which would also make them write the same file. The sources of another compiler (PareCompileSource) count too, so a .svelte and a .ts cannot share a URL. Paths that differ only in case count as the same, as they do on macOS and Windows file systems. The build stops before stripping anything. Give one of the named sources a different PareServeAt, or exclude one of them from TypeScriptSource (<TypeScriptSource Remove=\"Scripts/app.ts\" />) or PareCompileSource. PARE202 warning PARE202: not served: outside wwwroot with no PareServeAt, so the .js goes next to the source and is not a static web asset. Add PareServeAt=\"js\" to its item to serve it under js/, or add PARE202 to NoWarn to keep it as is. MSBuild package only, for web projects whose output goes under obj/. A TypeScriptSource (or PareCompileSource) item outside wwwroot, such as Scripts/app.ts, has no PareServeAt. Its output is written next to it, as Scripts/app.js, which the web app does not serve or publish. The build still succeeds. Not reported for PareOutDir, outside web projects, or for a .razor.ts or .cshtml.ts, whose output beside the component the SDK serves itself. To serve it, name the URL folder: <TypeScriptSource Include=\"Scripts/**/*.ts\" PareServeAt=\"js\" /> To keep the output next to the source on purpose, add <NoWarn>$(NoWarn);PARE202<\/NoWarn> to the project. PARE203 error PARE203: PareServeAt=\"js/app.js\" is not a URL folder. Give a folder such as js, or / for the root, with no . or .. segment, colon, double slash or file name: the file name comes from the source. MSBuild package only. PareServeAt names a folder; each file's name and extension come from its source (app.ts gives app.js, app.mts gives app.mjs). Values with a . or .. segment, a colon, // (or \\\\), or ending in a script extension such as .js or .ts are rejected. The build stops before stripping anything. Use a folder: PareServeAt=\"js\", or PareServeAt=\"/\" to serve at the root of the project's static web assets. PARE204 error PARE204: PareCompileSource needs PareCompiler (the compiler's name: letters, digits, . _ -) and PareOutputExtension (the output's extension, a dot and a name such as .svelte.js); got PareCompiler=\"husk\" PareOutputExtension=\"svelte.js\". MSBuild package only. A package that compiles another language to JavaScript (see Compiling other languages) declared a PareCompileSource item without a PareCompiler made of letters, digits, ., _ and -, or without a PareOutputExtension that starts with a dot and holds no folder or quote. The build stops before compiling anything. This is a fault in that package's .props or in the project file that added the item: <PareCompileSource Include=\"Components/**/*.svelte\" PareCompiler=\"husk\" PareOutputExtension=\".svelte.js\" />"},{"u":"06-reference-license.html","t":"License","s":"Reference","h":[],"b":"License Pare is licensed under the Islamic Software License - Permissive (ISL-P) v1.2, with the Output Exception (ISL-OE) v1.2. It is a source-available, ethically restricted license rooted in Islamic principles. It is not an OSI-approved open-source license. You can use Pare, including commercially, within the license's ethical use restrictions (Sections 4 and 5 of LICENSE.md). Read them before you adopt it. The Output Exception lets you ship the JavaScript Pare generates as part of your own work. The full text is LICENSE.md and the notices are NOTICE.md, both in the repository root. This page is a summary, not legal advice. The license text decides."},{"u":"06-reference-msbuild-properties.html","t":"MSBuild properties","s":"Reference","h":[{"i":"properties","t":"Properties"},{"i":"item-metadata","t":"Item metadata"},{"i":"items-you-can-read","t":"Items you can read"},{"i":"what-gets-stripped","t":"What gets stripped"},{"i":"where-the-output-goes","t":"Where the output goes"}],"b":"MSBuild properties Set these in the project file. The package is Extrode.Pare.Build. The task runs in-process under dotnet build, and in MSBuild's .NET task host (a separate process) under Visual Studio 2026. Properties Property Default EnablePareDefaultItems true false drops the default glob; add <TypeScriptSource Include=\"...\" /> yourself PareOutDir empty write under this directory, keeping relative paths, instead of obj/ or next to each source; not served, .razor.js and .cshtml.js included PareTypeCheck false true runs tsc --noEmit -p $(PareTsconfig) first (TypeScript 7's native tsc) PareTscPath tsc for example $(MSBuildThisFileDirectory).tools/typescript/tsc PareTsconfig tsconfig.json in the project never copied to the build or publish output PareKeepSources false true serves and publishes the .ts sources next to the output PareEmbed false true embeds every output in the assembly instead; see Non-web projects PareEmbedPrefix empty prepended to each embedded resource's name, for example web/ Item metadata On TypeScriptSource and PareCompileSource items: Metadata PareServeAt the URL folder to serve this source under; see TypeScript outside wwwroot PareCompiler PareCompileSource only: the compiler's name PareOutputExtension PareCompileSource only: the output's extension, such as .svelte.js Items you can read After the PareStripTypeScript target, @(PareOutput) lists every output. Each has %(PareSource) (the source's full path) and %(PareRelativePath), written with /: the URL path for a static web asset (js/app.js), the resource name without the prefix under PareEmbed, otherwise the output's path below the project (or below PareOutDir). A target with AfterTargets=\"PareStripTypeScript\" sees it on every build, including builds that skip the strip as up to date. What gets stripped Before Build, every wwwroot/**/*.ts and *.mts (not .d.ts, not node_modules) is stripped. .cts is not in the default glob, since CommonJS does not run in a browser. An explicit <TypeScriptSource Include=\"x.cts\" /> produces x.cjs. Where the output goes Situation Output web project, source in wwwroot obj/<configuration>/<tfm>/pare/js/app.js, registered as the static web asset js/app.js web project, source elsewhere, with PareServeAt the same place under obj/, served at the URL you chose source elsewhere, no PareServeAt the .js next to its source (warning PARE202) PareOutDir set below PareOutDir, not served PareEmbed true below obj/, embedded in the assembly Other details: .ts and .d.ts files are removed from Content, so they are not static web assets and are not published. PareKeepSources=true keeps them. In a web project without PareOutDir, the build deletes the output of a deleted source. Output written next to the source (a non-web project) or under PareOutDir stays; delete it yourself. A foo.js next to foo.ts in wwwroot (left by Pare 0.2, or hand-written) is not served; warning PARE200 names it. Delete it. Outside obj/, an existing foo.js next to foo.ts is replaced on the next build that strips foo.ts. Rejections fail the build as file(line,col): error PARE100: ...."},{"u":"06-reference-output-changes.html","t":"Output changes","s":"Reference","h":[{"i":"relative-import-extensions","t":"Relative import extensions"},{"i":"automatic-semicolon-insertion-fixes","t":"Automatic semicolon insertion fixes"},{"i":"everything-else","t":"Everything else"}],"b":"Output changes Pare's output differs from its input only by spaces, with these exceptions. Relative import extensions Relative module specifiers ./x.ts are rewritten to ./x.js (.mts to .mjs, .cts to .cjs) in import, export ... from and import(\"...\"). Where Default pare CLI on MSBuild package on library (StripOptions.RewriteRelativeImportExtensions) off, opt in C library (PARE_REWRITE_RELATIVE_IMPORT_EXTENSIONS) off, opt in Automatic semicolon insertion fixes Erasing a statement can leave two lines that JavaScript would join into one. Pare repairs these cases and no others: a ) moved to the end of an erased multi-line return type; a ( moved to the start of erased type parameters that are followed by a line break; a ; written at the start of an erased statement or class member when the previous line could otherwise merge with the next; a ; written at the end of an erased as or satisfies type when the next line starts with (, [ or a template; a ; written at the end of the erased type of a field named get, set or static. Everything else Spaces. The positions of all other characters, including comments and tabs, are the same as in your source. A comment inside an erased range, such as between a parameter list and its return type, is blanked with the rest of it."},{"u":"06-reference-supported-syntax.html","t":"Supported syntax","s":"Reference","h":[{"i":"erased","t":"Erased"},{"i":"kept-as-written","t":"Kept as written"},{"i":"refused","t":"Refused"},{"i":"not-supported","t":"Not supported"},{"i":"the-reference","t":"The reference"}],"b":"Supported syntax What Pare erases, and what it refuses. Erasing means replacing every character with a space, except line terminators (\\n, \\r, U+2028, U+2029), which are kept. Erased Construct Example Erased part Variable, parameter, return annotations let x: number, f(a: T): U {} : number, : T, : U Optional markers f(a?: T), { x?: T } in classes ? and the annotation Definite assignment let x!: T, x!: T; in a class !: T Non-null assertion obj!.x, fn()! ! as and satisfies x as T, x satisfies T as T, satisfies T Generic parameters function f<T>(), class C<T>, <T>(x) => x <T> Generic arguments f<T>(x), new Map<K, V>(), tag<T>`...` <T>, <K, V> interface declarations interface A { } the whole declaration type aliases type A = B; the whole declaration declare anything declare const x: T;, declare global {}, declare module \"x\" {} the whole declaration Type-only namespaces namespace N { type X = 1 } the whole declaration import type, export type import type { A } from \"x\" the whole statement Inline type specifiers import { type A, b } from \"x\" type A, Class modifiers public, private, protected, readonly, override the keyword abstract classes and members abstract class C, abstract m(): void; abstract, plus the whole abstract member implements clause class C implements I, J implements I, J this parameter function f(this: Window, a) this: Window, Overload signatures function f(a: string): void; with no body the whole signature Class index signatures [key: string]: any; the whole member Bodiless class members m(): void; in a class the whole member declare class fields declare x: T; the whole member export of erased declarations export interface A {}, export type A = B the whole statement, export included export { type A } type A and the trailing comma export type * from \"x\" the whole statement unique symbol, asserts x is T, infer, mapped and conditional types inside types covered by \"the type\" Kept as written Standard (TC39) decorators on classes and members. The runtime has to support them. declare enum, which emits nothing, is erased. declare namespaces and namespaces that hold only types are erased. Refused Code Construct Why PARE100 enum, const enum generates runtime code PARE101 namespace or module containing values generates runtime code PARE102 parameter properties: constructor(private x) generates this.x = x PARE103 import x = require(\"y\"), import x = N.y generates code PARE104 export = x CommonJS-only emit PARE105 angle-bracket assertion <T>expr ambiguous, and not allowed in .tsx; use as PARE106 parameter decorators need legacy experimentalDecorators PARE107 import type A, { B } from \"m\" TypeScript rejects it too (TS1363) Each has an example and the plain JavaScript to write in Error codes. Not supported .tsx and JSX. Syntax that needs type information to decide. For example, importing or re-exporting a type without the type keyword (import { Foo } from \"m\" where Foo is only a type) leaves a runtime import of a name that does not exist. Pare cannot know. Turn on verbatimModuleSyntax in tsconfig.json and tsc will make you write import type. The reference When there is a grammar question, TypeScript's parser.ts is the reference. On the TypeScript test corpus, Pare's output matches TypeScript 7 tsc emit (compared as syntax trees) for 99.96% of the files Pare strips. The rest are triaged differences on tsc's side."},{"u":"06-reference-troubleshooting.html","t":"Troubleshooting","s":"Reference","h":[{"i":"the-page-is-blank-or-the-script-404s","t":"The page is blank, or the script 404s"},{"i":"warning-pare200-not-served","t":"warning PARE200: not served"},{"i":"warning-pare202-not-served","t":"warning PARE202: not served"},{"i":"an-edit-does-not-show-up-under-dotnet-watch","t":"An edit does not show up under dotnet watch"},{"i":"the-build-says-error-pare100-to-pare107","t":"The build says error PARE100 to PARE107"},{"i":"error-pare001-syntax-error","t":"error PARE001: syntax error"},{"i":"a-type-error-went-through","t":"A type error went through"},{"i":"an-import-fails-at-run-time-but-the-file-stripped-fine","t":"An import fails at run time but the file stripped fine"},{"i":"visual-studio","t":"Visual Studio"},{"i":"linux-musl-arm64-and-nativeaot","t":"linux-musl-arm64 and NativeAOT"}],"b":"Troubleshooting The page is blank, or the script 404s Is the page asking for .js? The .ts is never served in a published site. Point your <script> at /js/app.js. Did the build run? Look for obj/<configuration>/<tfm>/pare/js/app.js. Did you call app.MapStaticAssets()? The build registers Pare's output as a static web asset, and MapStaticAssets serves it. With CreateSlimBuilder, also call builder.WebHost.UseStaticWebAssets(); without it an unpublished run answers requests that send Accept-Encoding with the build's compressed copies it cannot find, 200 and an empty body. Is / a 404 while /js/app.js works? Add app.UseDefaultFiles(); and app.UseRouting(); before app.MapStaticAssets();. warning PARE200: not served A foo.js sits next to foo.ts in wwwroot. Delete it (and any .gitignore rule for it). The build serves the stripped foo.ts from obj/. warning PARE202: not served A source outside wwwroot has no PareServeAt. Add one, or add PARE202 to NoWarn. See TypeScript outside wwwroot. An edit does not show up under dotnet watch Did you call builder.AddPareDevelopment()? Without it a .ts save runs no build. Run dotnet watch from the project directory, not with --project from elsewhere. A PublishAot project needs <StartupHookSupport Condition=\"'$(Configuration)' == 'Debug'\">true<\/StartupHookSupport>. A .ts file you added during the session is 404 until the next build. Press Ctrl+R. More in Hot reload. The build says error PARE100 to PARE107 Your TypeScript uses syntax that needs generated code. Error codes shows the plain JavaScript to write for each. Turn on \"erasableSyntaxOnly\": true in tsconfig.json so the editor warns you first. error PARE001: syntax error The file is not valid TypeScript at that position. Pare stops at the first syntax error. .tsx and JSX are not supported. A type error went through Pare does not check types. See Type checking in CI. An import fails at run time but the file stripped fine You imported a type without type: import { Foo } from \"./foo.ts\" where Foo is an interface. Pare cannot know, so it keeps the import. Write import type { Foo } or import { type Foo }, and set \"verbatimModuleSyntax\": true so tsc reminds you. Visual Studio Visual Studio 2026 (MSBuild 18) runs the task in MSBuild's .NET task host, a separate process. Visual Studio 2022 cannot build net10.0 projects at all. linux-musl-arm64 and NativeAOT .NET misreads the main thread's stack size there, so the deep-nesting check fails at once. Call TypeStripper.Strip (or pare_strip) from a thread you created."}];
